<h2>Overview: Two Approaches to AI Coding</h2>

<h3>Cursor: The Established Powerhouse</h3>
<p>Cursor has been the reference point for AI code editors since 2024. Built on VS Code, it offers deep integration with Claude and GPT models, an agent mode that can implement multi-file features, and the entire VS Code extension marketplace inherited from its base. Its tab completion is the feature competitors get measured against, and it regularly predicts multi-line edits rather than single tokens.</p>
<p>Cursor's agent mode is the standout. Beyond suggesting code, it can plan and implement changes across multiple files — create components, refactor imports, install dependencies, and run tests. The workflow it's built for is handing over a whole task rather than accepting suggestions line by line.</p>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Professional developers who need production-ready AI assistance with full control<br/><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free (Hobby) · Starts at $20/month (Pro) · $40/user/month (Business)</p>

<h3>Windsurf: The Context-Driven Challenger</h3>
<p><strong>Rating: 4.4/5 · <a href="/tool/windsurf">Read full review →</a></strong></p>
<p>Windsurf, built by Codeium and now owned by Cognition (the company behind Devin), took a different approach from the start. Rather than bolting AI onto a VS Code fork, Windsurf was designed AI-first. Its Cascade system uses a "Flows" model where the AI maintains persistent context about what you've been working on — in theory, it gets better the longer you stay in a session.</p>
<p>Arena mode lets you run multiple AI models side-by-side on the same task and compare outputs before accepting. Windsurf also introduced its own SWE-1.5 model optimized for agentic coding, reducing dependency on external AI providers. The March 2026 pricing redesign harmonized Pro at $20/month and introduced a $200/month Max tier for heavy users.</p>
<p>Notable: On July 14, 2025, Cognition (the company behind Devin) officially confirmed its acquisition of Windsurf — no longer a rumor. OpenAI had earlier explored acquiring Windsurf for $3 billion in May 2025, but the deal fell through. The acquisition signals deeper enterprise integrations ahead.</p>
<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Developers who want an AI that learns their patterns and prefer experimentation<br/><strong>Pricing:</strong> Free · $20/mo (Pro) · $200/mo (Max) · $40/user/mo (Teams)</p>

<h3>How We Score This Comparison</h3>
<p>Each category below is rated on two things you can check: <strong>documented capability</strong> (what the vendor ships, and at which plan) and <strong>reported day-to-day experience</strong> (the consistent pattern across developer discussion, not a single review). Weighting reflects what decides the purchase for most developers: <strong>Agent Mode Quality (25%)</strong> · <strong>Context Persistence (20%)</strong> · <strong>Speed (15%)</strong> · <strong>Pricing Value (15%)</strong> · <strong>UX (15%)</strong> · <strong>Ecosystem (10%)</strong>. Star ratings are editorial judgement on that evidence — not the output of a private benchmark.</p>

