<h2>Difference 1: Agent Mode — Multi-File Feature Implementation</h2>
<p><strong>Edge: Cursor</strong></p>
<p>The scenario that separates these two is a feature that touches ten files: add a dark mode toggle, create the context provider, persist the choice, and update every component that reads colour tokens. Cursor's agent is built to plan that whole change and then execute it — it maps which files need edits, creates new components, fixes imports, and can run your build to check itself. Windsurf's Cascade will happily attempt the same task, and does the central piece well, but the reported pattern is that it more often misses the dependent files that need updating alongside it, which turns one prompt into a short follow-up conversation.</p>
<p>That gap is smaller than it was in 2024, and it depends heavily on how well your codebase is structured — a feature that's cleanly separated by module is easier for either tool than one smeared across shared hooks.</p>

<h2>Difference 2: Context Persistence Across a Long Session</h2>
<p><strong>Edge: Windsurf</strong></p>
<p>This is where Windsurf's design choice pays off. Cascade's Flows model carries forward what it has learned about your project — naming conventions, the shape of your data layer, architectural decisions made an hour ago — so later requests inherit that instead of needing it restated. Cursor keeps context per session and per conversation; it's good at it, but a long refactoring day involves re-establishing more of the picture as you go.</p>
<p>The trade-off is real in both directions: persistent context is what you want on a multi-hour refactor and can be noise on a quick one-off script. If your work is mostly long sessions in one repo, weight this heavily. If you open many small projects, less so.</p>

<h2>Difference 3: Code Completion — Tab Prediction</h2>
<p><strong>Edge: Cursor</strong></p>
<p>Cursor's tab model is the feature the rest of the category is judged against, and it earns that reputation on multi-line prediction: it proposes the parameter additions, the follow-on function call, the boilerplate block, not just the next token. Windsurf's completion is genuinely good and has improved consistently, but developers who switch back and forth tend to report that it plays it safer — shorter completions that miss broader intent.</p>
<p>Latency is the part that's easiest to overstate. Both are fast enough to stay out of the way on modern hardware; if you're on an older laptop, try both before deciding, because this is the one dimension where your own machine matters more than the review.</p>

<h2>Difference 4: Debugging and Error Resolution</h2>
<p><strong>Edge: Tie</strong></p>
<p>Both handle diagnostics well, and the difference is stylistic rather than decisive. Cursor's in-editor suggestions during a debugging session tend to be more surgical, while Cascade more often gives you the explanation of why the error occurs — which is the more useful half if you're learning the codebase rather than just unblocking.</p>
<p>Neither will reliably find a race condition in a service you don't understand. What both do well is the middle band of work: reading a stack trace, locating the likely culprit, and proposing a fix you can evaluate.</p>

<h2>Difference 5: Extension and Ecosystem Support</h2>
<p><strong>Edge: Cursor</strong></p>
<p>Cursor inherits the full VS Code marketplace, so whatever extension your team already depends on will almost certainly work. Windsurf supports the popular ones and ships a curated set of its own, but niche extensions — the internal tooling plugin, the language server for that one legacy system — are where compatibility gaps appear. If your workflow leans on unusual extensions, verify those specific ones before committing.</p>

<h2>Difference 6: Arena / Model Flexibility</h2>
<p><strong>Edge: Windsurf</strong></p>
<p>Arena mode is Windsurf's clearest structural advantage: run the same task through several models side-by-side and take the better output. Cursor lets you switch models, but you're choosing on reputation rather than on the output in front of you. For teams where model choice is a live question — or where a new model ships every few weeks — Arena removes the guesswork. Windsurf's own SWE-1.5 model also means it isn't purely reselling someone else's inference.</p>

<h2>Difference 7: Onboarding and Setup</h2>
<p><strong>Edge: Windsurf</strong></p>
<p>Windsurf gets you to a first useful answer with less ceremony: it scans the project, suggests a model that suits the stack, and starts working. Cursor rewards configuration — once you've tuned the rules, chosen your models and set up your context files, it's the more powerful tool, but the path there is more deliberate. If you're evaluating on a Friday afternoon, that difference feels bigger than it is. If you'll use the editor for a year, it doesn't.</p>

